tel 1.0

base caduceus
import soundness

info   email composed from typed parts and sent through a courier chosen by what is in scope
detail
    _Caduceus_ composes an email from typed parts — a body of text, HTML or both, inline
    content and attachments — and sends it through a `Courier`, the delivery service, which
    is a contextual value. The MIME structure follows from the content (`multipart/alternative`
    for text with HTML, `multipart/related` with inlines, `multipart/mixed` with
    attachments), and the addresses are urticose's `EmailAddress`es, checked as the code
    compiles when written as `email"…"` literals.

    Anything `Sendable` — `Text`, a document with a media type such as an HTML page, or a
    composed `Email` — is sent with `send`, which takes the subject and recipients and needs
    a `Courier` and a `Sender` in scope:

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import couriers.resendCourier
    import internetAccess.online
    import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    given Sender = Sender(email"noreply@example.com")
    given Resend.ApiKey = Resend.ApiKey(t"re_123456789")

    val receipt: Resend.Receipt =
      t"Your order has shipped.".send(subject = t"Shipping", to = email"customer@example.com")
    ```

    The result of `send` is the courier's own `Result` type: the Resend courier returns a
    `Resend.Receipt`, and another courier may return something else, or `Unit`.

    The library has two source sets:

    - `caduceus.core` — `Email`, `Envelope`, `Sender`, `Courier`, the `Sendable` and
      `Attachable` typeclasses and the `send` extension. It needs a courier to do anything.
    - `caduceus.resend` — `Resend` and the `couriers.resendCourier` given, which delivers
      through the [Resend](https://resend.com/) HTTP API. JVM only, since it needs an HTTP
      client.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. A courier is selected by importing
    it by name from the `couriers` choice package, since `import couriers.*` does not import
    givens; the package merges with the umbrella's own.

refer caduceus.Email
refer caduceus.Courier
refer caduceus.send
refer caduceus.couriers
refer urticose.EmailAddress

entry .send   sends any `Sendable` value as an email, with a subject and recipients, through the `Courier` in scope
    `value.send(subject, to, cc, bcc, replyTo)` turns the value into an `Email` with its
    `Sendable`, builds an `Envelope` from the `Sender` in scope and the arguments, and hands
    both to the `Courier` in scope. Each of `to`, `cc`, `bcc` and `replyTo` takes either one
    `EmailAddress` or a `List` of them; all but `to` default to none.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import couriers.resendCourier
    import internetAccess.online
    import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    given Sender = Sender(email"reports@example.com")
    given Resend.ApiKey = Resend.ApiKey(t"re_123456789")

    t"Sales rose 4% in August.".send
      ( subject = t"Monthly report",
        to      = List(email"alice@example.com", email"bob@example.com"),
        cc      = email"archive@example.com" )
    ```

    The result type is the courier's `Result`, and failures are the courier's to report: the
    Resend courier raises `Courier.Error`.

entry .Sender   the address emails are sent from, as a contextual value
  detail
      `given Sender = Sender(email"noreply@example.com")`: `send` takes the envelope's `from`
      address from the `Sender` in scope, so it is chosen once for a program or a scope
      rather than repeated at every send.
  entry #email   the sender's address

entry .Envelope   who an email is from and to, and its subject: what a courier needs beside the message
  detail
      The metadata of a `Document[Email]`: the sender, the `to`, `cc`, `bcc` and `replyTo`
      recipients, and the subject. `send` builds it from its arguments and the `Sender` in
      scope; a `Courier` reads it from the document's `metadata`.
  entry .many   one value or a list of them, as a list
      `Envelope.many(address)` is `List(address)`, and `Envelope.many(addresses)` is the list
      itself. It is how `send` accepts either form for each recipient field.
  entry #from   the sender's address
  entry #to   the primary recipients
  entry #cc   the recipients of a copy
  entry #bcc   the recipients of a blind copy, not shown to the others
  entry #replyTo   the addresses a reply should go to, if not the sender
  entry #subject   the subject line

entry .Email   an email message: headers, a body of text or HTML or both, inline content and attachments
  detail
      An `Email` is a map of extra headers and an `Email.Message`, which nests an
      `Email.Content` (an `Email.Body` and any `Email.Inline`s) inside a list of
      attachments, each an `Asset`. Each layer reports its own MIME content type, derived
      from what it holds. An email is `Documentary` with an `Envelope` as its metadata, so a
      courier receives a `Document[Email]`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Text alone
        code
            import soundness.*

            val email = Email(t"Sales rose 4% in August.")
            email.message.contentType   // media"text/plain"
      frame
        label Text with HTML
        code
            import soundness.*

            val body = Email.Body(t"Sales rose 4%.", t"<p>Sales rose <b>4%</b>.</p>")
            val email = Email(Map(), Email.Message(Email.Content(body)))
            email.message.contentType   // media"multipart/alternative"
      frame
        label With an attachment
        code
            import soundness.*

            val body = Email.Body(t"Sales rose 4%.", t"<p>Sales rose <b>4%</b>.</p>")
            val report = Asset(t"report.csv", media"text/csv", Chain(t"month,sales".in[Data]))
            val email = Email(Map(), Email.Message(Email.Content(body))).attach(report)
            email.message.contentType   // media"multipart/mixed"
      ##
      ```

      An email reports its parts back with `#text`, `#html`, `#inlines` and `#attachments`,
      so a composed message can be checked before it is sent.
  refer caduceus.Sendable
  refer caduceus.Attachable
  refer caduceus.Envelope
  entry .apply   an email made from any `Sendable` value: `Email(t"hello")`
      Uses the value's `Sendable`: text becomes a plain-text body, an HTML document an
      HTML-only body, and an `Email` is itself. The result has no headers, inlines or
      attachments.
  entry .Body   the body of an email: text, HTML, or both as alternatives
    detail
        An enumeration of three cases: `TextOnly(content)`, `HtmlOnly(content)` and
        `Alternatives(textContent, htmlContent)`. The last is sent as
        `multipart/alternative`, so a client which can display HTML shows it and any other
        shows the text.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Email.Body(t"hello").contentType                   // media"text/plain"
        Email.Body.HtmlOnly(t"<p>hello</p>").contentType   // media"text/html"
        Email.Body(t"hello", t"<p>hello</p>").html         // t"<p>hello</p>"
        ```
    entry .apply   a text body, or text and HTML alternatives: `Email.Body(text, html)`
    entry #html   the HTML version, or `Unset` for a text-only body
    entry #text   the text version, or `Unset` for an HTML-only body
    entry #contentType   `text/plain`, `text/html` or `multipart/alternative`, by which versions the body has
  entry .Inline   content referred to from an HTML body by its content ID, such as an embedded image
    detail
        An inline part has a content ID, `cid`, which the HTML refers to as `cid:…`, a media
        type, and a body. Its presence makes the content `multipart/related`. The Resend
        courier does not send inline parts.
    entry #cid   the content ID the HTML refers to
    entry #contentType   the media type of the inline content
    entry #body   the inline content
  entry .Content   a body with any inline content it refers to
    detail
        `Email.Content(body, inlines*)`.
    entry #body   the text or HTML body
    entry #inlines   the inline parts the body refers to
    entry #contentType   `multipart/related` if there are inlines, and otherwise the body's type
  entry .Message   the content of an email with its attachments
    entry #content   the body and its inline parts
    entry #attachments   the attached files, in order
    entry #contentType   `multipart/mixed` if there are attachments, and otherwise the content's type
  entry #headers   additional headers, by name
  entry #message   the body, inline content and attachments
  entry #Self   the documentary type, `Email` itself
  entry #Metadata   the metadata of an email document: an `Envelope`
  entry #html   the HTML version of the body, or `Unset` if there is none
  entry #text   the text version of the body, or `Unset` if there is none
  entry #inlines   the inline parts of the content
  entry #attachments   the attached files, in the order they were attached
  entry #attach   the email with an attachment added at the end
      Takes any `Attachable` value: an `Asset`, or anything with a name, a media type and a
      byte stream. The email is immutable, so `attach` returns a new one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val report = Asset(t"report.csv", media"text/csv", Chain(t"month,sales".in[Data]))
      Email(t"The report is attached.").attach(report).attachments.map(_.name)
      ```

entry .Sendable   a typeclass for values which can be sent as an email
  detail
      `value.send(…)` and `Email(value)` accept any value with a `Sendable`, which turns it
      into an `Email`. Instances are given for `Text`, for `Email` itself, and for any
      document with a media type which streams as text, such as an HTML page from
      honeycomb. A new type is made sendable by mapping it to one that already is:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Report(title: Text, body: Text)

      given Report is Sendable = Sendable.text.contramap(_.body)
      ```
  entry .text   text is sent as a plain-text body
  entry .document   a document with a media type is sent as its text, HTML-only if it is `text/html`
      For any type with a `Media` and a `Streamable by Text`: a document whose media type is
      `text/html` becomes an `HtmlOnly` body, and any other a text body. The document is read
      in full when the email is made.
  entry .email   an email is sent as itself
  entry #email   the value as an email
  entry #contramap   a `Sendable` for another type, which converts it to this one first

entry .Attachable   a typeclass for values which can be attached to an email
  detail
      `Email#attach` takes any value with an `Attachable`, which turns it into an `Asset`: a
      name, a media type and the bytes. An `Asset` is attachable as itself, and any value
      which has a name (`Nominable`), a media type (`Media`) and a byte stream is attachable
      without further work.
  entry .generic   attaches any value which has a name, a media type and a byte stream
      For types with a `Nominable`, a `Media` and a `Streamable by Data`: the asset takes the
      value's name as its filename, its media type, and its bytes as a stream.
  entry .asset   an `Asset` is attached as itself
  entry #attachment   the value as an asset to attach
  entry #contramap   an `Attachable` for another type, which converts it to this one first

entry .Courier   a delivery service for email, chosen as a contextual value
  detail
      A `Courier` sends a `Document[Email]` — the email and its `Envelope` — and returns a
      `Result` of its own choosing. The provided one is `couriers.resendCourier`; another
      service plugs in by implementing the one method:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      class DryRunCourier() extends Courier:
        type Result = Text

        def send(message: Document[Email]): Text =
          t"would send ${message.metadata.subject} from ${message.metadata.from}"
      ```

      A courier is a capability: it is built from other capabilities (an HTTP client, a
      `Tactic`) and retains them.
  refer caduceus.couriers
  entry .Error   raised when a courier cannot deliver an email
    detail
        Error 383: _unable to send email from `from` to `to` with subject `subject`_. The
        Resend courier raises it for any failure to connect, an HTTP error status, or a
        response it cannot read. The `to` is the first of the envelope's primary recipients.
    entry #from   the sender's address
    entry #to   the first primary recipient's address
    entry #subject   the subject of the email
  entry #send   delivers an email with its envelope, returning the courier's `Result`

entry .Resend   the settings and results of the Resend courier
  detail
      [Resend](https://resend.com/) is an email-delivery service with an HTTP API. Its
      courier, `couriers.resendCourier`, takes its API key from a `Resend.ApiKey` given and
      returns a `Resend.Receipt` for each email sent.
  refer caduceus.couriers.resendCourier
  entry .ApiKey   the Resend API key, as a contextual value
    detail
        `given Resend.ApiKey = Resend.ApiKey(t"re_…")`. It is sent as a bearer token with
        every request. Read it from the environment or a secret store rather than writing it
        into the source.
    entry #key   the key, as text
  entry .Receipt   Resend's acknowledgement of an email it has accepted
    detail
        Decoded from the JSON response to a send. The ID identifies the email in Resend's
        API and dashboard.
    entry #id   the email's ID at Resend

entry .couriers   the provided email couriers, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A choice package, merged with the umbrella's `couriers`. Which service delivers email
      is decided by the `Courier` given in scope, and `import soundness.*` brings none in:
      one is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import couriers.resendCourier
      ```
  entry .resendCourier   delivers email through the Resend HTTP API, returning a `Resend.Receipt`
      Posts each email, with its text and HTML bodies, its extra headers and its
      attachments (encoded in Base64), to `https://api.resend.com/emails`, authorised by the
      `Resend.ApiKey` in scope. It needs a `Tactic[Courier.Error]`, `Online` (from
      `internetAccess`), an `Http.Client` (such as `httpBackends.javaNetHttp`) and the
      API key, and logs its HTTP requests as `Http.Event`s.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import couriers.resendCourier
      import internetAccess.online
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      given Sender = Sender(email"noreply@example.com")
      given Resend.ApiKey = Resend.ApiKey(t"re_123456789")

      Email(t"hello").send(subject = t"Greetings", to = email"friend@example.com").id
      ```

      A failed connection, an error status or an unreadable response raises
      `Courier.Error`; the underlying reason is printed to standard output first. Inline
      parts are not sent, and an email with no `to` recipient fails rather than raising.
